@author "Claude (Anthropic)"
@design traditional
@source "[tanfrog]"

; The traditional frog after the 19 steps of Kelly Tan's diagram
; [tanfrog], https://origami.me/traditional-frog/. Steps 1 to 5 fold the
; preliminary base, steps 6 to 12 turn it into the frog base by four squash
; folds and four petal folds, steps 13 to 18 narrow the four legs and
; reverse-fold them, and step 19 folds the head down.
;
; The program stops at the frog base, after step 12. The legs of steps 13
; to 19 are not folded yet (tophcodes/beloch#323).
;
; Smith writes the frog base in OIL from the preliminary base
; [smith1975oil, §5.4, p. 34]: one fold instruction per squash and three per
; petal fold. This program writes a squash as one statement and a petal
; fold as one application of `petal`.

paper square

; steps 9 and 10 on one squashed flap: --s is the crease of its squash, --e
; the paper edge whose middle .m the squash laid on the center line, .l and
; .r the corners at the ends of --e, .o the closed point. The flap is two
; layers deep at its sides: the top layer, which holds .m, and under it the
; layer hinged to it along --s at each side.
def petal(--s --e .l .r .m .o) {
  .ql = free on --e from .l at 1/8              ; a point in each lower layer
  .qr = free on --e from .r at 1/8
  .wl = (--s & #[.ql]) * --e                    ; the ends of the flap's sides
  .wr = (--s & #[.qr]) * --e
  --mid = (through .o .l)

  ; step 9: each bottom edge onto the center line and back, through both
  ; layers of the flap
  fold (map .wl onto --mid through .l) (moving .wl) (up to .ql) as --kl
  unfold (--kl) (moving .wl) (up to .ql)
  fold (map .wr onto --mid through .r) (moving .wr) (up to .qr) as --kr
  unfold (--kr) (moving .wr) (up to .qr)

  ; step 10: the petal fold, one fan at each point where a kite crease
  ; meets a side of the flap. The kite crease folds in the lower layer
  ; only; the top layer folds along the creases from that point to .m and
  ; along the crease between the two points, which the statement scores,
  ; and lifts .m in front. The top layer shows the back of the paper, so
  ; the creases to .m, which the diagram draws as mountains, read valley.
  .vl = (--kl & #[.ql]) * --s
  .vr = (--kr & #[.qr]) * --s
  mark (through .vl .m) (on #[.m]) as --ml
  mark (through .vr .m) (on #[.m]) as --mr
  flatten (fan at .vl (--s & .wl) (--kl & .l) (--ml valley))
          (fan at .vr (--s & .wr) (--kr & .r) (--mr))
}

; steps 1 to 3 crease the square in half both ways and along both
; diagonals. Step 2 turns the paper over and step 4 turns it back, so the
; program turns nothing: the square keeps the side up that step 1 starts
; with.
mark (map --da onto --bc) as --v                 ; step 1: in half and unfold
mark (map --ab onto --cd) as --h                 ; step 2: in half the other way
mark (through .a .c) as --ac                     ; step 3: one diagonal
mark (through .b .d) as --bd                     ; step 3: the other diagonal

; step 5: collapse into the preliminary base. All four corners meet at the
; open end, the paper center .o is the closed point, and the letters on
; three of its creases pick the state with the quarter of --ab in front.
flatten (--h & --bc) (--v & --cd valley) (--h & --da valley) (--v & --ab) (--bd & .b mountain) (--bd & .d)   ; step 5

.o = --h * --v
.ml = --v * --cd                                 ; the side corners
.mr = --v * --ab
.x6 = --h * --da                                 ; the ends of the folded edges
.x7 = --h * --bc                                 ; that steps 6 and 7 open

; points a quarter in from a corner that name the half of a squash a turn
; in step 8 moves
.k = free on --bc from .b at 1/4
.e = free on --da from .d at 1/4

; step 6: squash the front flap whose folded edge is --h & --da onto the
; center line --ac.
squash (--h & --da onto --ac) as --s1            ; step 6

; step 7: the model turned over, the same squash on the flap of the other
; side, whose folded edge is --h & --bc.
flip                                             ; step 7
squash (--h & --bc onto --ac) as --s2            ; step 7

; step 8, seen from the back: turn the half of the squash of step 7 that
; lies on the side of .k over to the other side, which uncovers the flap
; whose folded edge is --v & --ab, and squash that flap. Then, the model
; turned over, the same on the front: the half of the squash of step 6 on
; the side of .e over, and a squash of the flap whose folded edge is
; --v & --cd. Each turn runs along hinges the squash left and folds no new
; crease.
fold (--h & --bc) (moving .k) (up to .k)         ; step 8
squash (--v & --ab onto --bd) as --s3            ; step 8
flip                                             ; step 8
fold (--h & --da) (moving .e) (up to .e)         ; step 8
squash (--v & --cd onto --bd) as --s4            ; step 8

; steps 9 and 10 on the front flap, the squash of --v & --cd, and step 11
; on the back flap, the squash of --v & --ab
apply petal(--s4 --cd .d .c .ml .o)              ; steps 9 and 10
flip                                             ; step 11
apply petal(--s3 --ab .a .b .mr .o)              ; step 11

; points on the other halves of the squashes of steps 6 and 7, which the
; turns of step 12 move
.t1 = free on --da from .a at 3/8
.t2 = free on --bc from .c at 3/8

; step 12: turn the top two flaps on the right over to the left, which
; opens the hinge the matching turn of step 8 folded, lays the squash of
; step 6 flat in front and folds the petal on top in half along its
; center line. Then the petal fold on that squash, and the same on the
; back for the squash of step 7.
fold (--h & --da) (moving .t1) (up to .t1)       ; step 12
apply petal(--s1 --da .a .d .x6 .o)              ; step 12
flip                                             ; step 12
fold (--h & --bc) (moving .t2) (up to .t2)       ; step 12
apply petal(--s2 --bc .c .b .x7 .o)              ; step 12

; the base stands with its closed point up, as the diagram draws it from
; step 6 on
@orient .o up

; every write but a mark makes one step
; assert steps = 33

; the four petal folds and their creases cut the paper into 48 faces
; assert faces = 48

; the outline is the frog base: the closed point .o and the four paper
; corners at the open end
; assert .o = (1/2, 1/2)
; assert .a = (1, 1)
; assert .b = .a
; assert .c = .a
; assert .d = .a

; each petal fold lifts the middle of a paper edge to one point of the
; center line between .o and the corners. Its coordinates carry sqrt(2).
; assert .ml = .x6
; assert .mr = .x6
; assert .x7 = .x6
